  // ==========================================================================
  // 8.1 Elliptic Curves with Complex Multiplication and the Main Theorem of CM
  // ==========================================================================
  == Elliptic Curves with Complex Multiplication and the Main Theorem of CM

  An elliptic curve $E$ over the complex numbers $bb(C)$ is analytically isomorphic to a complex torus $bb(C) \/ Lambda$, where $Lambda = bb(Z) omega_1 + bb(Z) omega_2 subset.eq bb(C)$ is a lattice of rank $2$ over $bb(Z)$ with $tau = omega_1 \/ omega_2$ in the upper half-plane $cal(H) = { z in bb(C) : op("Im")(z) > 0 }$. Every holomorphic endomorphism of $bb(C) \/ Lambda$ fixing the origin is induced by multiplication by a complex number $alpha in bb(C)$ satisfying $alpha Lambda subset.eq Lambda$. Thus the endomorphism ring of $E$ is canonically identified with
  $ op("End")(E) tilde.equiv { alpha in bb(C) : alpha Lambda subset.eq Lambda }. $
  For a generic lattice $Lambda$, the only multipliers preserving $Lambda$ are the rational integers $bb(Z)$. When $op("End")(E)$ is strictly larger than $bb(Z)$, we say that $E$ has *complex multiplication* (CM).

  #proposition(title: "Endomorphism Rings of Complex Elliptic Curves")[
    Let $E \/ bb(C) tilde.equiv bb(C) \/ Lambda$ be a complex elliptic curve. Then either $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv bb(Z)$, or $op("End")(E)$ is isomorphic to an order $cal(O)$ in an imaginary quadratic field $K = bb(Q)(sqrt(-d))$ ($d > 0$ squarefree).
  ]

  #proof[
    Suppose $alpha in op("End")(E) without bb(Z)$. Since $alpha Lambda subset.eq Lambda$ and $Lambda$ is a free $bb(Z)$-module of rank $2$, multiplication by $alpha$ is represented by a $2 times 2$ integer matrix $M_alpha in M_2 (bb(Z))$ with respect to a $bb(Z)$-basis $(omega_1, omega_2)$ of $Lambda$. By the Cayley–Hamilton theorem, $alpha$ satisfies the monic quadratic equation
    $ alpha^2 - op("tr")(M_alpha) alpha + det(M_alpha) = 0. $
    Because $Lambda$ spans $bb(C)$ over $bb(R)$ and $alpha in.not bb(R)$ (if $alpha in bb(R)$ preserved $Lambda$, then $alpha omega_1 = a omega_1 + b omega_2$ with $tau = omega_1 \/ omega_2 in.not bb(R)$ would force $b = 0$ and $alpha = a in bb(Z)$), the roots $alpha, overline(alpha)$ are non-real complex conjugates. Hence $K = bb(Q)(alpha)$ is an imaginary quadratic field. Every $beta in op("End")(E)$ lies in $K$ (since $beta tau in bb(Q) + bb(Q) tau = K$) and is integral over $bb(Z)$ (being an eigenvalue of $M_beta in M_2 (bb(Z))$). Thus $op("End")(E)$ is a subring of the ring of integers $cal(O)_K$ that is free of rank $2$ over $bb(Z)$, i.e., an order in $K$.
  ]

  Throughout this chapter we focus primarily on the *maximal order* case $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$, where $K$ is an imaginary quadratic field of fundamental discriminant $D_K < 0$ (so $D_K = -d < 0$ with $d = |D_K|$). Let $w = |cal(O)_K^times|$ denote the number of roots of unity in $K$ ($w = 4$ for $K = bb(Q)(i)$, $w = 6$ for $K = bb(Q)(sqrt(-3))$, and $w = 2$ for all other $K$), and let $h = |op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)|$ be the class number of $K$.

  === Ideal Class Action and Integrality of the $j$-Invariant

  Fix an embedding $K arrow.r.hook bb(C)$. If $E \/ bb(C) tilde.equiv bb(C) \/ Lambda$ satisfies $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$, then choosing any nonzero $omega in Lambda$ yields $omega^(-1) Lambda subset.eq K$, which is a finitely generated $cal(O)_K$-submodule of $K$—that is, a *fractional ideal* $frak(a)$ of $K$. Two lattices $Lambda_1, Lambda_2$ define isomorphic complex elliptic curves if and only if $Lambda_2 = c Lambda_1$ for some $c in bb(C)^times$, which corresponds precisely to equality of ideal classes $[frak(a)_1] = [frak(a)_2]$ in $op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$.

  #definition(title: "Ideal Class Action on CM Elliptic Curves")[
    Let $op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)$ denote the set of $bb(C)$-isomorphism classes of elliptic curves $E \/ bb(C)$ with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$. The ideal class group $op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$ acts on $op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)$ by
    $ [frak(a)] star (bb(C) \/ frak(b)) := bb(C) \/ (frak(a)^(-1) frak(b)). $
    This action is *simply transitive*, so $|op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)| = h = |op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)|$.
  ]

  The inversion $frak(a)^(-1)$ in the definition is standard and ensures compatibility with the Artin reciprocity map of class field theory. Algebraically, if $frak(a) subset.eq cal(O)_K$ is an integral ideal, the kernel of the natural isogeny $bb(C) \/ frak(b) -> bb(C) \/ frak(a)^(-1) frak(b)$ is the finite subgroup $E[frak(a)] = { P in E(bb(C)) : alpha(P) = 0 text(" for all ") alpha in frak(a) }$, so $[frak(a)] star E tilde.equiv E \/ E[frak(a)]$.

  A complex elliptic curve $E \/ bb(C) tilde.equiv bb(C) \/ Lambda$ is classified up to $bb(C)$-isomorphism by its *modular $j$-invariant*
  $ j(E) = j(Lambda) = 1728 g_2 (Lambda)^3 / (g_2 (Lambda)^3 - 27 g_3 (Lambda)^2), $
  where $g_2 (Lambda) = 60 sum_(omega in Lambda without {0}) omega^(-4)$ and $g_3 (Lambda) = 140 sum_(omega in Lambda without {0}) omega^(-6)$ are the Eisenstein invariants of the Weierstrass equation $y^2 = 4 x^3 - g_2 x - g_3$. In terms of $q = e^(2 pi i tau)$, $j(tau)$ has the $q$-expansion
  $ j(tau) = q^(-1) + 744 + 196884 q + 21493760 q^2 + dots.c in bb(Z)((q)). $
  For a CM elliptic curve, $j(E)$ is not merely algebraic—it is an *algebraic integer*, a foundational fact with far-reaching consequences for reduction theory.

  #theorem(title: "Algebraicity and Integrality of CM j-Invariants")[
    Let $E \/ bb(C)$ be an elliptic curve with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$. Then:
    + $j(E)$ is an algebraic integer, and $[bb(Q)(j(E)) : bb(Q)] <= h$.
    + Every Galois conjugate of $j(E)$ over $bb(Q)$ is of the form $j(E')$ for some $E' in op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)$.
  ]

  #proof[
    For any automorphism $sigma in op("Aut")(bb(C))$, twisting the defining Weierstrass equation of $E$ by $sigma$ produces an elliptic curve $E^sigma$ with $j(E^sigma) = sigma(j(E))$ and $op("End")(E^sigma) tilde.equiv op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$. Since $op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)$ has finite cardinality $h$, the orbit of $j(E)$ under $op("Aut")(bb(C))$ has size at most $h$. Therefore $j(E)$ is algebraic over $bb(Q)$ of degree at most $h$, and its conjugates all lie in ${ j(E') : E' in op("Ell")(cal(O)_K) }$.

    To prove that $j(E)$ is an algebraic *integer*, recall the classical modular polynomial $Phi_m (X, Y) in bb(Z)[X, Y]$ of level $m >= 1$:
    $ Phi_m (X, j(tau)) = product_(A in op("SL")_2 (bb(Z)) backslash M_2 (bb(Z))_m) (X - j(A tau)), $
    where $M_2 (bb(Z))_m$ denotes the set of primitive $2 times 2$ integer matrices of determinant $m$. By the $q$-expansion principle and symmetric polynomial theory, $Phi_m (X, Y)$ has coefficients in $bb(Z)$, is monic in $X$ of degree $psi(m) = m product_(p divides m)(1 + 1\/p)$, and satisfies $Phi_m (j(E_1), j(E_2)) = 0$ if and only if there is a cyclic isogeny $E_1 -> E_2$ of degree $m$.

    Choose a primitive element $alpha in cal(O)_K without bb(Z)$ of norm $m = N_(K \/ bb(Q))(alpha) = alpha overline(alpha)$ such that $m$ is not a square in $bb(Z)$ (for instance, take $alpha = sqrt(-d)$ if $d$ is not a square, or $alpha = 1 + sqrt(-1)$ of norm $2$ for $K = bb(Q)(i)$, or $alpha = 1 + 2 omega$ of norm $3$ for $K = bb(Q)(sqrt(-3))$). Then multiplication by $alpha$ defines a cyclic endomorphism of $E$ of degree $m$, so $Phi_m (j(E), j(E)) = 0$.

    We claim that when $m$ is not a square, the univariate polynomial $F_m (X) = Phi_m (X, X) in bb(Z)[X]$ has leading coefficient $plus.minus 1$. Every matrix $A in op("SL")_2 (bb(Z)) backslash M_2 (bb(Z))_m$ has a unique upper-triangular representative
    $ A = mat(a, b; 0, d), quad a d = m, quad 0 <= b < d, quad gcd(a, b, d) = 1. $
    In $bb(Z)[zeta_m]((q^(1 \/ m)))$, we have $j(A tau) = zeta_m^(-a b) q^(-a \/ d) + O(1)$, whereas $j(tau) = q^(-1) + O(1)$. Since $m$ is not a square, we always have $a != d$, so the leading term of $j(tau) - j(A tau)$ as $q -> 0$ is either $q^(-1)$ (if $a < d$) or $-zeta_m^(-a b) q^(-a \/ d)$ (if $a > d$). In both cases the leading coefficient is a root of unity! Taking the product over all $A$, the leading coefficient of $F_m (j(tau))$ as a Laurent series in $q^(1 \/ m)$ is a root of unity that also lies in $bb(Z)$ (since $F_m in bb(Z)[X]$ and $j(tau) = q^(-1) + O(1)$), hence equals $plus.minus 1$. Thus $plus.minus F_m (X) in bb(Z)[X]$ is monic and annihilates $j(E)$, proving that $j(E)$ is an algebraic integer.
  ]

  === The Main Theorem of Complex Multiplication and Ray Class Fields

  The integrality of $j(E)$ already hints that CM elliptic curves are intimately bound up with the arithmetic of $K$. The full relationship is governed by the *Main Theorem of Complex Multiplication*, originally envisioned by Kronecker, developed by Weber, Takagi, Fueter, and Hasse, and cast into its definitive idelic form by Shimura and Taniyama.

  Let $bb(A)_K^times$ denote the idele group of $K$, and let $bb(A)_(K, f)^times$ be its finite part. Global class field theory provides the global Artin reciprocity map
  $ op("Art")_K : bb(A)_K^times \/ K^times -> op("Gal")(K^(op("ab")) \/ K), $
  normalized (following the geometric/Shimura convention) so that a uniformizer $pi_frak(p)$ at a finite prime $frak(p)$ maps to the arithmetic Frobenius element $op("Frob")_frak(p)$ on unramified extensions. For a finite idele $s in bb(A)_(K, f)^times$ and a fractional ideal $frak(a) subset.eq K$, we define the fractional ideal $s frak(a)$ locally by $(s frak(a))_frak(p) = s_frak(p) frak(a)_frak(p)$ for every finite prime $frak(p)$ of $K$. Multiplication by $s$ induces an isomorphism of $cal(O)_K$-modules
  $ s : K \/ frak(a) ->^(tilde.equiv) K \/ s frak(a), $
  since $K \/ frak(a) tilde.equiv plus.o_frak(p) K_frak(p) \/ frak(a)_frak(p)$ is a torsion module on which finite ideles act componentwise.

  #theorem(title: "Main Theorem of Complex Multiplication for Elliptic Curves")[
    Let $K$ be an imaginary quadratic field embedded in $bb(C)$, let $E \/ bb(C)$ be an elliptic curve with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$, and let $xi : bb(C) \/ frak(a) ->^(tilde.equiv) E(bb(C))$ be an analytic uniformization for a fractional ideal $frak(a)$ of $K$, normalized so that $op("End")(E)$ acts via $alpha dot.c xi(z) = xi(alpha z)$. Let $sigma in op("Aut")(bb(C) \/ K)$, and choose an idele $s in bb(A)_K^times$ whose Artin symbol $op("Art")_K (s)$ agrees with $sigma |_(K^(op("ab")))$. Then there exists a unique analytic isomorphism
    $ xi' : bb(C) \/ s^(-1) frak(a) ->^(tilde.equiv) E^sigma (bb(C)) $
    such that the following diagram commutes on torsion points:
    $ xi'(s^(-1) z) = sigma(xi(z)) quad text("for all ") z in K \/ frak(a). $
  ]

  This theorem simultaneously determines *both* the Galois action on the curve $E$ itself (namely, $E^sigma tilde.equiv bb(C) \/ s^(-1) frak(a) = [(s)] star E$) and the Galois action on every torsion point $E(bb(C))_(op("tors")) = xi(K \/ frak(a))$. In the Lean 4 formalization `leanprover-community/riemann-hypothesis`, this exact idelic statement is formalized as `GlobalTheory.Published.EllipticMainTheoremCM` and proved in `Development/AutomorphicSpectrum/Proofs/GlobalProofs/EllipticMainTheoremCM.lean`.

  From the Main Theorem of CM, Kronecker's *Jugendtraum* follows in two celebrated corollaries. Recall that the *Weber function* $h : E -> E \/ op("Aut")(E) tilde.equiv bb(P)^1$ is the canonical quotient map by the automorphism group of $E$: for $y^2 = 4 x^3 - g_2 x - g_3$,
  $ h(x, y) = cases(
    (g_2 g_3 \/ Delta) dot.c x & text("if ") w = 2 quad (j != 0, 1728),
    (g_2^2 \/ Delta) dot.c x^2 & text("if ") w = 4 quad (j = 1728),
    (g_3 \/ Delta) dot.c x^3 & text("if ") w = 6 quad (j = 0),
  ) $
  where $Delta = g_2^3 - 27 g_3^2$ is the discriminant. The Weber function is independent of the choice of Weierstrass model and satisfies $h(P) = h(Q)$ if and only if $Q = zeta P$ for some $zeta in op("Aut")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K^times$.

  #corollary(title: "Hilbert Class Field and Ray Class Fields")[
    Let $E \/ bb(C)$ be an elliptic curve with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$, and let $n >= 1$ be an integer.
    + *Hilbert class field* (`HilbertClassFieldJ`): The field $H_K = K(j(E))$ is the maximal unramified abelian extension of $K$ (the Hilbert class field of $K$). In particular, $[H_K : K] = [bb(Q)(j(E)) : bb(Q)] = h$, and for every prime ideal $frak(p)$ of $K$, the Frobenius automorphism $op("Frob")_frak(p) in op("Gal")(H_K \/ K)$ satisfies
      $ op("Frob")_frak(p) (j(E)) = j([frak(p)] star E) = j(E \/ E[frak(p)]). $
    + *Ray class field* (`WeberRayFieldSt`): Adjoining the Weber coordinates of all $n$-torsion points generates the ray class field $K_((n))$ of $K$ modulo the ideal $(n) = n cal(O)_K$:
      $ K_((n)) = K(j(E), h(E[n])). $
  ]

  #proof[
    For (1), an automorphism $sigma in op("Aut")(bb(C) \/ K)$ fixes $j(E)$ if and only if $E^sigma tilde.equiv E$. By the Main Theorem of CM, $E^sigma tilde.equiv bb(C) \/ s^(-1) frak(a)$, which is isomorphic to $bb(C) \/ frak(a)$ if and only if the fractional ideal $(s)$ is principal. Under the global Artin map, the subgroup of ideles $s in bb(A)_K^times$ with $(s)$ principal is $K^times dot.c (bb(C)^times times product_frak(p) cal(O)_(K, frak(p))^times)$, whose fixed field in $K^(op("ab"))$ is precisely the Hilbert class field $H_K$.

    For (2), an automorphism $sigma in op("Aut")(bb(C) \/ H_K)$ fixes $h(E[n])$ if and only if $sigma$ fixes $E$ up to isomorphism and multiplies $E[n] tilde.equiv n^(-1) frak(a) \/ frak(a)$ by an automorphism $zeta in cal(O)_K^times$. By the commutative diagram $xi'(s^(-1) z) = sigma(xi(z))$, writing $(s) = (alpha)$ for $alpha in K^times$, this holds if and only if $alpha^(-1) s in cal(O)_(K, frak(p))^times$ is congruent to a unit $zeta in cal(O)_K^times$ modulo $n cal(O)_(K, frak(p))$ at every prime $frak(p)$ dividing $n$—that is, $s in K^times dot.c U_((n))$, the congruence subgroup defining the ray class field $K_((n))$.
  ]

  === Reduction of CM Elliptic Curves and Deuring's Criterion

  The integrality of $j(E)$ has an immediate geometric consequence for the reduction of $E$ modulo primes. Let $E$ be defined over a number field $L supset.eq H_K$ (in fact, one can always choose a model of $E$ over $H_K$ itself), and let $frak(P)$ be a finite prime of $L$ lying over a rational prime $p$. Because $j(E) in cal(O)_L$, the $frak(P)$-adic valuation of $j(E)$ is nonnegative: $v_frak(P) (j(E)) >= 0$. By the Semistable Reduction Theorem (or the explicit Kodaira–Néron classification of fibers), an elliptic curve with integral $j$-invariant has *potential good reduction* at $frak(P)$: after passing to a finite extension $L' \/ L$, $E$ admits a Weierstrass model over $cal(O)_(L', frak(P)')$ with unit discriminant $Delta in cal(O)_(L', frak(P)')^times$.

  More sharply, by the Serre–Tate criterion of good reduction (and the existence of the Grossencharacter $psi_(E \/ L)$ attached to $E$), if $p$ is unramified in $K$ (or more generally, at every prime $frak(P)$ where the Grossencharacter $psi_(E \/ L)$ is unramified), $E$ has good reduction at $frak(P)$ over an unramified extension of $L_frak(P)$. Moreover, when $p$ is inert in $K$, the ideal $p cal(O)_K$ is principal, so $p$ splits completely in $H_K \/ K$. Let us record this key fact explicitly, as it is vital for our local computations in Sections 8.3 and 8.4:

  #lemma(title: "Splitting of Inert Primes in the Hilbert Class Field")[
    Let $p$ be a rational prime that is inert in the imaginary quadratic field $K$. Then the prime ideal $frak(p) = p cal(O)_K$ splits completely in the Hilbert class field $H_K \/ K$. Consequently, for every prime $frak(P)$ of $H_K$ lying above $p$, the completion $(H_K)_frak(P)$ is isomorphic to the unramified quadratic extension $K_p$ of $bb(Q)_p$, with ring of integers $cal(O)_(K_p) = W(bb(F)_(p^2))$ and uniformizer $p$.
  ]

  #proof[
    Since $p$ is inert in $K$, the ideal $frak(p) = p cal(O)_K$ is principal, hence trivial in $op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$. By Artin reciprocity, the Frobenius element $op("Frob")_frak(p) in op("Gal")(H_K \/ K) tilde.equiv op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$ is the identity, so $frak(p)$ splits completely in $H_K \/ K$. Thus $(H_K)_frak(P) = K_frak(p) = K_p$, which is the unique unramified quadratic extension of $bb(Q)_p$.
  ]

  Now suppose $E$ has good reduction at a prime $frak(P) mid(|) p$ of a number field $L supset.eq H_K$, with special fiber $tilde(E)$ over the finite residue field $k = cal(O)_L \/ frak(P) tilde.equiv bb(F)_q$ ($q = p^f$). Recall that an elliptic curve $tilde(E)$ over $bb(F)_q$ is called *ordinary* if $tilde(E)[p](overline(bb(F))_p) tilde.equiv bb(Z) \/ p bb(Z)$ and *supersingular* if $tilde(E)[p](overline(bb(F))_p) = {0}$. Deuring's criterion completely characterizes which type occurs for a CM elliptic curve in terms of the splitting of $p$ in $K$.

  #theorem(title: "Deuring's Criterion for CM Reduction")[
    Let $E \/ L$ be an elliptic curve with $op("End")_L (E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$ having good reduction at a prime $frak(P)$ of $L$ lying over a rational prime $p$. Let $tilde(E)$ be the reduction of $E$ modulo $frak(P)$.
    + $tilde(E)$ is *ordinary* if and only if $p$ splits in $K$, i.e., $p cal(O)_K = frak(p) overline(frak(p))$ with $frak(p) != overline(frak(p))$.
    + $tilde(E)$ is *supersingular* if and only if $p$ is *inert* or *ramified* in $K$, i.e., there is a unique prime ideal of $cal(O)_K$ lying over $p$.
  ]

  #proof[
    Reduction modulo $frak(P)$ induces an injective ring homomorphism
    $ cal(O)_K tilde.equiv op("End")_L (E) arrow.r.hook op("End")_(overline(bb(F))_p) (tilde(E)). $
    Moreover, let $pi_q in op("End")_(bb(F)_q) (tilde(E))$ be the $q$-power Frobenius endomorphism of $tilde(E)$, where $q = |k| = p^f$. Since every endomorphism in $cal(O)_K$ is defined over $L$, its reduction commutes with $pi_q$.

    *Case 1: $tilde(E)$ is ordinary.* Then $op("End")_(overline(bb(F))_p)(tilde(E)) times.o_bb(Z) bb(Q)$ is an imaginary quadratic field, so the injection $K arrow.r.hook op("End")_(overline(bb(F))_p)(tilde(E)) times.o bb(Q)$ is an isomorphism. Hence $pi_q in cal(O)_K$, and its norm is $N_(K \/ bb(Q))(pi_q) = deg(pi_q) = q = p^f$. Because $tilde(E)$ is ordinary, the trace of Frobenius $a_q = pi_q + overline(pi)_q in bb(Z)$ is coprime to $p$. Therefore the principal ideals $(pi_q)$ and $(overline(pi)_q)$ of $cal(O)_K$ are coprime: any prime ideal $frak(p)$ dividing both would divide $pi_q + overline(pi)_q = a_q$ as well as $pi_q overline(pi)_q = p^f$, contradicting $gcd(a_q, p) = 1$. Since $(pi_q)(overline(pi)_q) = (p)^f$ with $(pi_q)$ and $(overline(pi)_q)$ coprime and conjugate, $p cal(O)_K$ must factor into two distinct conjugate prime ideals $frak(p) overline(frak(p))$. Thus $p$ splits in $K$.

    *Case 2: $tilde(E)$ is supersingular.* Then $B = op("End")_(overline(bb(F))_p)(tilde(E)) times.o_bb(Z) bb(Q)$ is the unique definite quaternion algebra over $bb(Q)$ ramified precisely at $p$ and $oo$. By the Albert–Brauer–Hasse–Noether theorem, a quadratic field $K$ embeds into $B$ if and only if $K_v$ does not split $B_v$ at every place $v$ where $B$ is ramified—that is, $K_oo = bb(C)$ (so $K$ is imaginary quadratic) and $K times.o_bb(Q) bb(Q)_p$ is a field (so $p$ does not split in $K$). Equivalently, $p$ is inert or ramified in $K$.
  ]

  Deuring's criterion gives us our first glimpse of the special role played by *inert primes*: when $p$ is inert in $K$, the prime $p$ simultaneously splits completely in $H_K \/ K$ (giving a pristine unramified local ring $cal(O)_(K_p)$ with uniformizer $p$) *and* forces the reduction $tilde(E)$ to be supersingular!

  // ==========================================================================
  // 8.2 The Chowla–Selberg Formula and CM Periods
  // ==========================================================================
  == The Chowla–Selberg Formula and CM Periods

  To pass between analytic functions on the complex torus $bb(C) \/ Lambda$ (such as theta functions and their derivatives) and algebraic numbers in $H_K$ (whose $p$-adic valuations we wish to estimate), we must normalize by the *fundamental period* $Omega$ of the CM elliptic curve. Remarkably, the archimedean magnitude of $Omega$ is given in closed form by a product of special values of Euler's Gamma function at rational arguments—the *Chowla–Selberg formula*.

  === Normalization of the Fundamental Period

  Let $E$ be an elliptic curve defined over a number field $L supset.eq H_K$ with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$, and fix a nowhere-vanishing invariant differential $omega in H^0 (E, Omega^1_(E \/ L))$ (for a Weierstrass model $y^2 + a_1 x y + a_3 y = x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_4 x + a_6$, we take $omega = d x \/ (2 y + a_1 x + a_3)$). For a chosen complex embedding $L arrow.r.hook bb(C)$, the period lattice
  $ Lambda_(E, omega) = { integral_gamma omega : gamma in H_1 (E(bb(C)), bb(Z)) } subset.eq bb(C) $
  is a rank-$2$ lattice with $op("End")(Lambda_(E, omega)) = cal(O)_K$. Therefore there exists a fractional ideal $frak(a)$ of $K$ and a nonzero complex number $Omega in bb(C)^times$, uniquely determined up to multiplication by $K^times$ (and up to $cal(O)_K^times$ once $frak(a)$ is fixed), such that
  $ Lambda_(E, omega) = Omega dot.c frak(a). $
  We call $Omega$ a *fundamental CM period* of $K$. Notice that the ratio $Omega_1 \/ Omega_2$ of periods attached to any two curves $E_1, E_2 in op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)$ (or two algebraic differentials on $E$) is an *algebraic* number (in fact, up to algebraic factors, $Omega$ depends only on the imaginary quadratic field $K$, not on the ideal class $[frak(a)]$).

  #example(title: "The Lemniscate and Equianharmonic Periods")[
    + For $K = bb(Q)(i)$ ($D_K = -4$, $w = 4$, $h = 1$), consider the Gauss lemniscate curve $E : y^2 = 4 x^3 - 4 x$ with $omega = d x \/ y$. The real period is
      $ Omega = 2 integral_1^oo (d x) / sqrt(4 x^3 - 4 x) = 1 / 2 B(1 \/ 4, 1 \/ 2) = Gamma(1 \/ 4)^2 / (2 sqrt(2 pi)), $
      where $B(u, v) = Gamma(u) Gamma(v) \/ Gamma(u + v)$ is Euler's Beta function and $Gamma(1 \/ 2) = sqrt(pi)$. The period lattice is $Lambda = Omega dot.c bb(Z)[i]$.
    + For $K = bb(Q)(sqrt(-3))$ ($D_K = -3$, $w = 6$, $h = 1$), consider the equianharmonic curve $E : y^2 = 4 x^3 - 4$ with $omega = d x \/ y$. The real period is
      $ Omega = 2 integral_1^oo (d x) / sqrt(4 x^3 - 4) = 1 / 3 B(1 \/ 6, 1 \/ 2) = Gamma(1 \/ 3)^3 / (2^(4 \/ 3) pi), $
      using the Legendre duplication formula and reflection formula for $Gamma$, and the period lattice is $Lambda = Omega dot.c bb(Z)[(1 + sqrt(-3)) \/ 2]$.
  ]

  Notice that in both examples, $Omega$ is a rational power of $pi$ times a product of values $Gamma(a \/ |D_K|)$ weighted by the quadratic Dirichlet character $chi_K (a) = (D_K \/ a)$! The Chowla–Selberg formula (originally discovered in 1897 by Lerch and rediscovered in 1967 by Chowla and Selberg) generalizes this phenomenon to *every* imaginary quadratic field $K$.

  === Statement and Conceptual Derivation of the Chowla–Selberg Formula

  Let $K$ be an imaginary quadratic field of fundamental discriminant $D_K = -d < 0$ ($d = |D_K|$), with Kronecker character $chi_K (a) = (D_K \/ a)$ modulo $d$, class number $h$, and unit count $w = |cal(O)_K^times|$. Recall the Dedekind eta function
  $ eta(tau) = q^(1 \/ 24) product_(n >= 1) (1 - q^n), quad q = e^(2 pi i tau), quad tau in cal(H), $
  which satisfies $Delta(tau) = (2 pi)^12 eta(tau)^24$. For each ideal class $[frak(a)] in op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$, choose an integral basis $frak(a) = bb(Z) alpha_1 + bb(Z) alpha_2$ with $tau_frak(a) = alpha_1 \/ alpha_2 in cal(H)$. Note that the real quantity $op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2$ is invariant under $op("SL")_2 (bb(Z))$ and depends only on the ideal class $[frak(a)]$.

  #theorem(title: "Chowla–Selberg Formula")[
    Let $K$ be an imaginary quadratic field of fundamental discriminant $-d < 0$, class number $h$, unit count $w$, and quadratic character $chi_K = (-d \/ dot.c)$.
    + *(Dedekind eta form)* Averaging over all ideal classes $[frak(a)] in op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$,
      $ product_([frak(a)] in op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)) (op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2) = (2 pi d)^(-h \/ 4) product_(a=1)^(d-1) Gamma(a \/ d)^(w chi_K (a) \/ 4). $
    + *(CM period form)* Let $E \/ overline(bb(Q))$ be an elliptic curve with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$ and fundamental period $Omega$. Then up to a nonzero algebraic number $alpha_E in overline(bb(Q))^times$,
      $ Omega = alpha_E dot.c sqrt(pi) (product_(a=1)^(d-1) Gamma(a \/ d)^(w chi_K (a) \/ (2 h)))^(1 \/ 2), $
      or equivalently, writing $Omega_K = (2 pi \/ sqrt(d))^(1 \/ 2) (product_(a=1)^(d-1) Gamma(a \/ d)^(w chi_K (a) \/ (2 h)))^(1 \/ 2)$ for the canonical CM period, we have $Omega \/ Omega_K in overline(bb(Q))^times$, and for a minimal integral model over $H_K$ the geometric mean of the periods over the Galois orbit satisfies
      $ & (product_(sigma : H_K arrow.r.hook bb(C)) |Omega_(E^sigma)|)^(1 \/ [H_K : bb(Q)]) \
        & quad = |N_(H_K \/ bb(Q)) (Delta_E)|^(-1 \/ (12 [H_K : bb(Q)])) \
        & quad quad dot.c (2 pi / sqrt(d))^(1 \/ 2) (product_(a=1)^(d-1) Gamma(a \/ d)^(w chi_K (a) \/ (2 h)))^(1 \/ 2). $
  ]

  #proof[
    We give the complete conceptual derivation in three steps, as this argument illuminates how analytic $L$-values produce geometric periods.

    *Step 1: Epstein zeta functions and Kronecker's first limit formula.* For each ideal class $[frak(a)] in op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)$, let $Q_frak(a) (m, n) = N(m alpha_1 + n alpha_2) \/ N(frak(a))$ be the associated positive-definite primitive binary quadratic form of discriminant $-d$, where $tau_frak(a) = alpha_1 \/ alpha_2 in cal(H)$. Its Epstein zeta function is
    $ Z_(Q_frak(a)) (s) = sum_((m, n) in bb(Z)^2 without {(0, 0)}) Q_frak(a) (m, n)^(-s) quad (op("Re")(s) > 1). $
    By *Kronecker's first limit formula* (proved by applying Poisson summation in $m$ to $sum_(m, n) |m tau + n|^(-2 s)$ and expanding the resulting Bessel $K$-function series around $s = 1$), $Z_(Q_frak(a)) (s)$ admits a meromorphic continuation to $bb(C)$ with a simple pole at $s = 1$ and Laurent expansion
    $ Z_(Q_frak(a)) (s) &= (2 pi) / sqrt(d) dot.c 1 / (s - 1) \
      & quad + (4 pi) / sqrt(d) (gamma - log 2 - log(op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2)) + O(s - 1), $
    where $gamma$ is the Euler–Mascheroni constant.

    *Step 2: Summing over ideal classes and factoring $zeta_K (s)$.* Each nonzero integral ideal $frak(b)$ in the class $[frak(a)]^(-1)$ corresponds to $w$ pairs $(m, n) in bb(Z)^2 without {(0, 0)}$ with $Q_frak(a)(m, n) = N(frak(b))$. Summing over all $h$ ideal classes therefore yields
    $ sum_([frak(a)] in op("Cl")(cal(O)_K)) Z_(Q_frak(a)) (s) = w zeta_K (s) = w zeta(s) L(s, chi_K). $
    Expanding both sides at $s = 1$ using $zeta(s) = (s - 1)^(-1) + gamma + O(s - 1)$ and $L(s, chi_K) = L(1, chi_K) + L'(1, chi_K)(s - 1) + O((s - 1)^2)$, we match the residues to recover Dirichlet's class number formula $w L(1, chi_K) = 2 pi h \/ sqrt(d)$, and match the constant terms to obtain
    $ & w (gamma L(1, chi_K) + L'(1, chi_K)) \
      & quad = (4 pi) / sqrt(d) (h (gamma - log 2) - sum_([frak(a)]) log(op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2)). $
    Dividing by $w L(1, chi_K) = 2 pi h \/ sqrt(d)$ cancels the $gamma$ terms cleanly and gives
    $ 1 / h sum_([frak(a)]) log(op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2) = -log 2 - 1 / 2 (gamma + L'(1, chi_K) / L(1, chi_K)). $

    *Step 3: Evaluating $L'(1, chi_K) \/ L(1, chi_K)$ via the functional equation and Lerch's formula.* Recall the completed Dirichlet $L$-function for the odd primitive character $chi_K$ of conductor $d$:
    $ Lambda(s, chi_K) = (d / pi)^((s + 1) \/ 2) Gamma((s + 1) \/ 2) L(s, chi_K) = Lambda(1 - s, chi_K). $
    Taking logarithmic derivatives at $s = 1$ (and using $Gamma'(1) \/ Gamma(1) = -gamma$, $Gamma'(1 \/ 2) \/ Gamma(1 \/ 2) = -gamma - 2 log 2$) yields
    $ L'(1, chi_K) / L(1, chi_K) = -L'(0, chi_K) / L(0, chi_K) - log(d / pi) - gamma - 2 log 2, $
    so $gamma + L'(1, chi_K) \/ L(1, chi_K) = -L'(0, chi_K) \/ L(0, chi_K) - log(d \/ pi) - 2 log 2$. Now express $L(s, chi_K)$ in terms of the Hurwitz zeta function $zeta(s, x) = sum_(n >= 0) (n + x)^(-s)$:
    $ L(s, chi_K) = d^(-s) sum_(a=1)^(d-1) chi_K (a) zeta(s, a \/ d). $
    Differentiating at $s = 0$ and applying *Lerch's identity* $partial_s zeta(0, x) = log Gamma(x) - (1 \/ 2) log(2 pi)$ together with $sum_(a=1)^(d-1) chi_K (a) = 0$ and $L(0, chi_K) = 2 h \/ w$, we obtain
    $ L'(0, chi_K) = -L(0, chi_K) log d + sum_(a=1)^(d-1) chi_K (a) log Gamma(a \/ d). $
    Substituting this into Step 2 gives
    $ & 1 / h sum_([frak(a)]) log(op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2) \
      & quad = -1 / 4 log(2 pi d) + w / (4 h) sum_(a=1)^(d-1) chi_K (a) log Gamma(a \/ d), $
    which exponentiates directly to part (1).

    Finally, to deduce the period form (2), observe that for an elliptic curve $E \/ H_K$ with discriminant $Delta_E$ and period lattice $Lambda = Omega_E frak(a) = Omega_E alpha_2 (bb(Z) tau_frak(a) + bb(Z))$, the modularity of the discriminant gives
    $ Delta_E = Delta(Lambda) = (Omega_E alpha_2)^(-12) (2 pi)^12 eta(tau_frak(a))^24. $
    Taking absolute values and noting that $op("Im")(tau_frak(a)) = sqrt(d) N(frak(a)) \/ (2 |alpha_2|^2)$, we have
    $ |Omega_E|^(-12) |Delta_E|^(-1) dot.c |alpha_2|^(-12) = (2 pi)^(-12) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^(-24) $
    —or, rearranging cleanly,
    $ |Omega_E|^2 &= (2 pi)^2 |Delta_E|^(-1 \/ 6) |alpha_2|^(-2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^4 \
      &= (2 pi)^2 (2 / sqrt(d)) |Delta_E|^(-1 \/ 6) N(frak(a))^(-1) (op("Im")(tau_frak(a))^(1 \/ 2) |eta(tau_frak(a))|^2)^2. $
    Taking the geometric mean over the ideal classes (and using that $product_([frak(a)]) N(frak(a))$ is rational while the $E^sigma$ run over $op("Ell")(cal(O)_K)$) transforms part (1) directly into part (2).
  ]

  === Archimedean Size, $p$-Adic Valuation, and Algebraic Normalization

  The Chowla–Selberg formula plays a dual role in the Alpöge–Claude proof, bridging the archimedean and non-archimedean worlds:

  + *Archimedean control.* At the infinite place, the derivatives of a theta function $theta(z)$ at $z = 0$ are bounded analytically by Cauchy's integral formula or explicit Gaussian decay. When we divide $D^a theta(0)$ by $Omega^a$ to obtain an *algebraic* number, we must control the archimedean norm $|Omega|^(-a)$. By the Chowla–Selberg formula, $log |Omega|$ is explicitly controlled by $log d$ and $L'(1, chi_K) \/ L(1, chi_K)$, which determines the archimedean contribution to the Arakelov height (Chapter 9) of the normalized derivatives.

  + *Algebraic normalization and $p$-adic valuation.* Because $Omega$ itself is a *transcendental* complex number, it does not possess a $p$-adic valuation on its own. Why, then, can we speak meaningfully of $v_p (D^a theta(0) \/ Omega^a)$? The answer lies in the comparison between the *analytic* uniformization $bb(C) \/ Lambda tilde.equiv E(bb(C))$ and the *algebraic* model $cal(E) \/ cal(O)_(L, frak(P))$.

    On the complex torus $bb(C) \/ Lambda$ with coordinate $z$, the differential $d z$ is translation-invariant, and the algebraic invariant differential $omega = d x \/ (2 y)$ on the Weierstrass model pulls back to $omega = d z$ when $Lambda = Lambda_(E, omega) = Omega cal(O)_K$. If instead we parametrize the torus by the *dimensionless* coordinate $u = z \/ Omega in bb(C) \/ cal(O)_K$, then
    $ omega = d z = Omega dot.c d u, $
    and dually, the algebraic invariant derivation $D = (d \/ omega)$ on the model $cal(E)$ corresponds analytically to
    $ D = d / (d z) = 1 / Omega dot.c d / (d u). $
    Now let $f$ be a regular function on a formal neighborhood of the origin of $cal(E)$ (for example, a ratio of theta functions normalized so that its Taylor expansion in the local parameter $T = -x \/ y$ has algebraic—indeed, $frak(P)$-integral—coefficients). Writing $tilde(f)(u) = f(Omega u)$ for the function in the dimensionless coordinate $u in bb(C) \/ cal(O)_K$, the $a$-th derivative satisfies
    $ D^a f(0) = (d / (d z))^a f(0) = 1 / Omega^a (d / (d u))^a tilde(f)(0). $
    Thus the ratio $(1 \/ Omega^a) (d \/ d u)^a tilde(f)(0)$ is *literally* the algebraic quantity $(D^a f)(0) in L$! When $f$ is normalized integrally at $frak(P)$, its $frak(P)$-adic valuation $v_frak(P) ((D^a f)(0)) = v_frak(P) (D^a theta(0) \/ Omega^a)$ is completely well-defined—and, as we are about to prove via formal groups, it grows linearly in $a$ with exact slope $p \/ (p^2 - 1)$ whenever $p$ is inert in $K$.

  // ==========================================================================
  // 8.3 Formal Groups, Witt Vectors, and the Artin–Hasse Exponential
  // ==========================================================================
  == Formal Groups, Witt Vectors, and the Artin–Hasse Exponential

  To uncover the exact $p$-adic divisibility of $(D^a f)(0)$ at an inert prime $p$, we zoom in on an infinitesimal neighborhood of the origin $O in cal(E)$ over the local ring $cal(O)_(K_p)$. The group law of $cal(E)$ in a local uniformizer $T$ at $O$ defines a one-dimensional *formal group law* $hat(E)$ over $cal(O)_(K_p)$.

  === Formal Group Laws, Formal Logarithms, and Height

  #definition(title: "One-Dimensional Commutative Formal Group Law")[
    Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unity. A (one-dimensional, commutative) *formal group law* over $R$ is a power series $F(X, Y) in R[[X, Y]]$ satisfying:
    + $F(X, Y) = X + Y + sum_(i, j >= 1) a_(i, j) X^i Y^j$ (identity: $F(X, 0) = X$ and $F(0, Y) = Y$);
    + $F(X, F(Y, Z)) = F(F(X, Y), Z)$ (associativity);
    + $F(X, Y) = F(Y, X)$ (commutativity).

    A *homomorphism* $phi : F -> G$ of formal group laws over $R$ is a power series $phi(T) in T R[[T]]$ such that $phi(F(X, Y)) = G(phi(X), phi(Y))$. It is a *strict isomorphism* if $phi'(0) = 1$.
  ]

  The two simplest examples are the *additive* formal group $hat(bb(G))_a (X, Y) = X + Y$ and the *multiplicative* formal group $hat(bb(G))_m (X, Y) = X + Y + X Y$. For an elliptic curve $cal(E)$ over $R$ given by a Weierstrass equation $y^2 + a_1 x y + a_3 y = x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_4 x + a_6$, setting $T = -x \/ y$ and $w = -1 \/ y$ expresses $w(T) in T^3 R[[T]]$ uniquely as a power series in $T$, and the geometric group law on $cal(E)$ induces a formal group law $hat(E)(X, Y) in R[[X, Y]]$.

  For every integer $n in bb(Z)$, the multiplication-by-$n$ endomorphism $[n]_F (T) in T R[[T]]$ is defined inductively by $[0]_F (T) = 0$, $[n + 1]_F (T) = F([n]_F (T), T)$, and $[-1]_F (T)$ is the unique formal inverse satisfying $F(T, [-1]_F (T)) = 0$. One checks immediately that $[n]_F (T) = n T + O(T^2)$.

  Over a ring $R$ in which all positive integers are invertible (i.e., a $bb(Q)$-algebra), every one-dimensional formal group law is strictly isomorphic to $hat(bb(G))_a$ via its *formal logarithm*.

  #proposition(title: "Existence and Uniqueness of the Formal Logarithm")[
    Let $F$ be a formal group law over a $bb(Q)$-algebra $R$ (or more generally, let $R$ be a torsion-free ring and work in $(R times.o_bb(Z) bb(Q))[[T]]$). There exists a unique power series
    $ log_F (T) = sum_(n >= 1) c_n T^n / n in (R times.o_bb(Z) bb(Q))[[T]] $
    with $c_1 = 1$ such that $log_F (F(X, Y)) = log_F (X) + log_F (Y)$. Moreover, its formal derivative $log'_F (T) = sum_(n >= 1) c_n T^(n - 1)$ lies in $R[[T]]$ (so every $c_n in R$), and the differential $omega_F = log'_F (T) d T$ is the unique normalized translation-invariant differential on $F$.
  ]

  #proof[
    Differentiating the identity $log_F (F(X, Y)) = log_F (X) + log_F (Y)$ with respect to $Y$ and setting $Y = 0$ gives
    $ log'_F (X) dot.c (partial_2 F)(X, 0) = log'_F (0) = 1. $
    Since $F(X, Y) = X + Y + O(X Y)$, we have $(partial_2 F)(X, 0) = 1 + O(X) in R[[X]]^times$. Thus $log'_F (T) = ((partial_2 F)(T, 0))^(-1) in R[[T]]$ is uniquely determined and has coefficients $c_n in R$. Integrating term-by-term in $(R times.o_bb(Z) bb(Q))[[T]]$ with constant term $0$ gives $log_F (T) = sum_(n >= 1) c_n T^n \/ n$. Conversely, associativity of $F$ implies that $omega_F = ((partial_2 F)(T, 0))^(-1) d T$ is invariant under translation $T |-> F(T, Y)$, so $d(log_F (F(X, Y)) - log_F (X) - log_F (Y)) = 0$ in $(R times.o bb(Q))[[X, Y]]$, and comparing constant terms gives $log_F (F(X, Y)) = log_F (X) + log_F (Y)$.
  ]

  The inverse power series $exp_F (z) in (R times.o bb(Q))[[z]]$, satisfying $log_F (exp_F (z)) = z$ and $exp_F (log_F (T)) = T$, is the *formal exponential* of $F$. While $log_F$ and $exp_F$ linearize $F$ in characteristic $0$, the *denominators* of their coefficients carry profound $p$-adic information governed by the *height* of the reduction modulo $p$.

  #definition(title: "Height of a Formal Group Law in Characteristic p")[
    Let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p > 0$ and let $F$ be a formal group law over $k$. If $[p]_F (T) != 0$, then $[p]_F (T)$ is a power series in $T^p$ (since $[p]'_F (T) = p dot.c ((partial_2 F)(T, 0)) \/ ((partial_2 F)([p]_F (T), 0)) = 0$ in $k[[T]]$). In fact, there exists a unique integer $h >= 1$ and a power series $g(T) in k[[T]]$ with $g'(0) != 0$ such that
    $ [p]_F (T) = g(T^(p^h)) = u T^(p^h) + O(T^(p^h + 1)), quad u in k^times. $
    The integer $h$ is called the *height* of $F$. (If $[p]_F (T) = 0$, as for $hat(bb(G))_a$, one sets $h = oo$.)
  ]

  For the formal group $hat(E)$ of an elliptic curve $tilde(E)$ over $bb(F)_q$, the degree of the multiplication-by-$p$ isogeny $[p] : tilde(E) -> tilde(E)$ is $p^2$. The separable degree of $[p]$ equals $|tilde(E)[p](overline(bb(F))_p)|$, while the inseparable degree is $p^h$ where $h$ is the height of $hat(E)$. Hence:
  - $tilde(E)$ is *ordinary* ($|tilde(E)[p](overline(bb(F))_p)| = p$) if and only if $hat(E)$ has *height $1$*;
  - $tilde(E)$ is *supersingular* ($|tilde(E)[p](overline(bb(F))_p)| = 1$) if and only if $hat(E)$ has *height $2$*.

  Combining this with Deuring's Criterion (Theorem 8.7), we obtain the fundamental dichotomy for a CM elliptic curve $E$ with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$ at a prime $p$ unramified in $K$:
  - If $p$ *splits* in $K$, then $hat(E)$ has height $1$ over $bb(F)_p$.
  - If $p$ is *inert* in $K$, then $hat(E)$ has height $2$ over $bb(F)_(p^2)$.

  === Lubin–Tate Formal Groups and Honda's Classification

  To compute explicitly with height-$2$ formal groups over $cal(O)_(K_p)$, we do not need the messy Weierstrass expansion of $hat(E)$ itself—it suffices to know $hat(E)$ up to *strict isomorphism* over $cal(O)_(K_p)$, because a strict isomorphism $phi(T) = T + O(T^2) in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ preserves both the ring of integral power series $cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ and the normalized invariant derivation $D$! This is where Lubin–Tate theory and Honda's classification (via Hazewinkel's Functional Equation Lemma) enter.

  #definition(title: "Lubin–Tate Formal Group Law")[
    Let $L$ be a finite extension of $bb(Q)_p$ with ring of integers $cal(O)_L$, uniformizer $pi$, and residue field $bb(F)_q$ ($q = p^f$). Let $cal(F)_pi$ be the set of power series $e(T) in cal(O)_L[[T]]$ satisfying
    $ e(T) equiv pi T (mod T^2) quad text("and") quad e(T) equiv T^q (mod pi). $
    For each $e in cal(F)_pi$, there exists a unique formal group law $F_e (X, Y) in cal(O)_L[[X, Y]]$ admitting $e$ as an endomorphism (so $e(F_e (X, Y)) = F_e (e(X), e(Y))$). Moreover, $F_e$ acquires a canonical $cal(O)_L$-module structure $a |-> [a]_(F_e) (T) in cal(O)_L[[T]]$ with $[pi]_(F_e) = e$, and any two choices $e_1, e_2 in cal(F)_pi$ yield strictly isomorphic formal $cal(O)_L$-modules over $cal(O)_L$.
  ]

  Take $L = K_p$, the unramified quadratic extension of $bb(Q)_p$ (when $p$ is inert in $K$), with uniformizer $pi = p$ and $q = p^2$. A particularly clean choice of $e in cal(F)_p$ is $e(T) = p T + T^(p^2)$, or even better, the formal group $F_p$ whose logarithm is the pure $p^2$-typical series
  $ log_(F_p) (T) = sum_(k >= 0) T^(p^(2 k)) / p^k = T + T^(p^2) / p + T^(p^4) / p^2 + dots.c . $
  How do we know that $F_p (X, Y) = log_(F_p)^(-1)(log_(F_p)(X) + log_(F_p)(Y))$ actually has *integral* coefficients in $bb(Z)_p[[X, Y]]$? This is a direct instance of the *Hazewinkel Functional Equation Lemma*, which is also the engine behind Honda's classification of formal groups over unramified extensions of $bb(Z)_p$.

  #theorem(title: "Hazewinkel Functional Equation Lemma")[
    Let $K_v$ be a complete non-archimedean valued field of characteristic $0$ with ring of integers $cal(O)_v$, prime $p$ with $|p|_v < 1$, $q = p^h$ ($h >= 1$), and an endomorphism $sigma : K_v -> K_v$ lifting the $q$-power Frobenius: $|sigma(a) - a^q|_v <= |p|_v$ for all $a in cal(O)_v$. Suppose $f(T) in K_v[[T]]$ satisfies $f(0) = 0$, $f'(0) = 1$, and the *functional equation condition*
    $ f(T) - 1 / p (sigma_* f)(T^q) in cal(O)_v[[T]]. $
    Then:
    + *(Group integrality)* The formal group law $F(X, Y) = f^(-1)(f(X) + f(Y))$ has integral coefficients: $F(X, Y) in cal(O)_v[[X, Y]]$.
    + *(Homomorphism integrality)* If $g(T) in T K_v[[T]]$ also satisfies $g(T) - p^(-1) (sigma_* g)(T^q) in cal(O)_v[[T]]$, then $phi(T) = f^(-1)(g(T))$ has integral coefficients: $phi(T) in cal(O)_v[[T]]$.
  ]

  #proof[
    We prove (2) first; (1) follows by applying the same argument in two variables $X, Y$ to $g(X, Y) = f(X) + f(Y)$. Write $h(T) = f(T) - p^(-1) (sigma_* f)(T^q) in cal(O)_v[[T]]$ and $tilde(h)(T) = g(T) - p^(-1) (sigma_* g)(T^q) in cal(O)_v[[T]]$. We prove by induction on $n >= 1$ that the coefficient $c_n$ of $T^n$ in $phi(T) = f^(-1)(g(T))$ lies in $cal(O)_v$.

    For $n = 1$, $c_1 = g'(0) in cal(O)_v$ since $tilde(h)'(0) = g'(0) in cal(O)_v$. Now assume $c_1, dots.c, c_(n - 1) in cal(O)_v$, and let $phi_(n - 1)(T) = sum_(j = 1)^(n - 1) c_j T^j in cal(O)_v[T]$. By the functional equation,
    $ f(phi(T)) &= h(phi(T)) + 1 / p (sigma_* f)(phi(T)^q) \
      &= g(T) = tilde(h)(T) + 1 / p (sigma_* g)(T^q). $
    Since $phi(T) equiv phi_(n - 1)(T) + c_n T^n (mod T^(n + 1))$ and $h(T) = T + O(T^2) in cal(O)_v[[T]]$, we have
    $ h(phi(T)) equiv h(phi_(n - 1)(T)) + c_n T^n (mod T^(n + 1)). $
    Furthermore, because $phi_(n - 1)(T) in cal(O)_v[T]$ and $|sigma(a) - a^q|_v <= |p|_v$ for all $a in cal(O)_v$, we have the key congruence
    $ phi(T)^q equiv phi_(n - 1)(T)^q equiv (sigma_* phi_(n - 1))(T^q) (mod p cal(O)_v[[T]], T^(n + 1)). $
    Crucially, for any $u(T), v(T) in cal(O)_v[[T]]$ with $u(T) equiv v(T) (mod p)$, the binomial expansion gives $(u(T)^m - v(T)^m) \/ m in p cal(O)_v[[T]]$ for every $m >= 1$ (since $v_p (p^k \/ k) >= 1$ for $k >= 2$, or more simply because $v_p (binom(m, k) p^k) >= v_p (m) + 1$ for all $k >= 1$!). Applying this to the terms of $sigma_* f$ (whose $m$-th coefficient has denominator dividing $p^(v_p (m))$—indeed, by induction on the functional equation, $m$ times the $m$-th coefficient of $f$ lies in $cal(O)_v$), we deduce that
    $ 1 / p ((sigma_* f)(phi(T)^q) - (sigma_* f)((sigma_* phi_(n - 1))(T^q))) in cal(O)_v[[T]] quad (mod T^(n + 1)). $
    Now observe that $(sigma_* f)((sigma_* phi_(n - 1))(T^q)) = sigma_* (f(phi_(n - 1)(T)))(T^q)$, and since $f(phi_(n - 1)(T)) equiv g(T) (mod T^n)$, their $q$-th expansions agree modulo $T^(q n)$—hence certainly modulo $T^(n + 1)$ (as $q n >= 2 n >= n + 1$). Therefore
    $ 1 / p (sigma_* f)(phi(T)^q) - 1 / p (sigma_* g)(T^q) in cal(O)_v[[T]] quad (mod T^(n + 1)). $
    Comparing the coefficient of $T^n$ in $f(phi(T)) = g(T)$ now immediately yields $c_n in cal(O)_v$, completing the induction!
  ]

  In `Formalism/FormalGroupLog/Statement.lean`, this exact theorem is formalized as `Formalism.G.FunEqGroup` and `Formalism.G.FunEqHom` under the predicate `IsFunEqType`, and its multidimensional vector generalization (for formal group laws of arbitrary dimension $g$ with a Frobenius matrix $M$) is formalized in `Formalism/FormalGroupWitt/Statement.lean` as `Formalism.FG.FunEqHomV`!

  #corollary(title: "Strict Isomorphism of CM Formal Groups at Inert Primes")[
    Let $E$ be an elliptic curve with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$ having good reduction at a prime $frak(P)$ of $H_K$ lying over an inert prime $p$ of $K$. Then over $cal(O)_(K_p)$, the formal group $hat(E)$ is strictly isomorphic to the canonical height-$2$ Lubin–Tate formal group $F_p$ with logarithm
    $ log_(F_p) (T) = sum_(k >= 0) T^(p^(2 k)) / p^k. $
    In particular, there exists a strict isomorphism $phi(T) = T + O(T^2) in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ such that
    $ log_(hat(E)) (T) = sum_(k >= 0) (phi(T))^(p^(2 k)) / p^k. $
  ]

  #proof[
    Since $p$ is inert in $K$, the residue field of $K_p$ is $bb(F)_(p^2)$ and the endomorphism ring $op("End")(hat(E))$ contains $cal(O)_(K_p)$ with $[p]_(hat(E))(T) equiv u T^(p^2) (mod p)$ for a unit $u in cal(O)_(K_p)^times$. Over the maximal unramified extension $W(overline(bb(F))_p)$, we may rescale the coordinate $T |-> v T$ by a unit $v in W(overline(bb(F))_p)^times$ solving $v^(p^2 - 1) = u$ so that $[p]_(hat(E))(T) equiv T^(p^2) (mod p)$ (making $hat(E)$ a Lubin–Tate formal $cal(O)_(K_p)$-module for the uniformizer $p$ and $q = p^2$). By Honda's classification, both $log_(hat(E))$ and $log_(F_p)$ satisfy the functional equation condition `IsFunEqType` for $q = p^2$ with $sigma$ lifting the $p^2$-power Frobenius. By Theorem 8.14(2), the composition $phi(T) = log_(F_p)^(-1)(log_(hat(E))(T))$ lies in $W(overline(bb(F))_p)[[T]]$ (and over $cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ whenever $u = 1$) with $phi'(0) = 1$, establishing the strict isomorphism.
  ]

  === Witt Vectors and the Artin–Hasse Exponential

  To connect formal groups, characteristic-$0$ lifts, and higher-dimensional generalizations seamlessly, we introduce the *ring of $p$-typical Witt vectors* $W(R)$ and the *Artin–Hasse exponential* $E_p (X)$.

  For a prime $p$ and any commutative ring $R$, the Witt polynomials are
  $ w_n (X_0, X_1, dots.c, X_n) = sum_(i=0)^n p^i X_i^(p^(n - i)) in bb(Z)[X_0, dots.c, X_n] quad (n >= 0). $
  There exist unique polynomials $S_n, P_n in bb(Z)[X_0, dots.c, X_n; Y_0, dots.c, Y_n]$ such that the *ghost map* $w = (w_0, w_1, dots.c) : W(R) -> R^bb(N)$ is a ring homomorphism when $W(R) = R^bb(N)$ is equipped with addition $S$ and multiplication $P$. For a perfect field $k$ of characteristic $p$, $W(k)$ is the unique complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic $0$ with residue field $k$ and uniformizer $p$; in particular, $W(bb(F)_p) = bb(Z)_p$, $W(bb(F)_(p^2)) = cal(O)_(K_p)$, and $W(overline(bb(F))_p)$ is the completion of the maximal unramified extension of $bb(Z)_p$ (`Formalism.FG.WittCharZero`). Every element $x in k$ admits a unique *Teichmüller lift* $[x] = (x, 0, 0, dots.c) in W(k)$ satisfying $[x y] = [x][y]$; in particular, $W(overline(bb(F))_p)$ contains primitive $(p^d - 1)$-st roots of unity for every $d >= 1$ (`Formalism.FG.WittTeichRoot`).

  Closely intertwined with $p$-typical Witt vectors is the *Artin–Hasse exponential series*:
  $ E_p (X) := exp(sum_(k >= 0) X^(p^k) / p^k) in bb(Q)[[X]]. $
  At first glance, the coefficients of $E_p (X)$ look hopelessly non-integral because $exp(z) = sum z^n \/ n!$ introduces $p$-powers in denominators on top of the $1 \/ p^k$ factors inside the exponent. Yet miraculously, every single $p$-power denominator cancels!

  #theorem(title: "Integrality of the Artin–Hasse Exponential")[
    For every prime $p$, the Artin–Hasse exponential $E_p (X)$ has $p$-adic integral coefficients:
    $ E_p (X) = exp(sum_(k >= 0) X^(p^k) / p^k) in bb(Z)_p[[X]]. $
    Moreover, $E_p (X)$ admits the infinite product factorization
    $ E_p (X) = product_(p divides.not m) (1 - X^m)^(-mu(m) \/ m), $
    where $mu$ is the Möbius function and the product runs over all positive integers $m$ coprime to $p$.
  ]

  #proof[
    We give two complementary proofs, each highlighting a different facet of $p$-adic integrality.

    *Proof 1 (Möbius product).* Using $-log(1 - Y) = sum_(r >= 1) Y^r \/ r$, compute the formal logarithm of the infinite product $P(X) = product_(p divides.not m) (1 - X^m)^(-mu(m) \/ m)$:
    $ log P(X) = sum_(p divides.not m) mu(m) / m sum_(r >= 1) X^(m r) / r = sum_(n >= 1) X^n / n sum_(m divides n, p divides.not m) mu(m). $
    Write $n = p^k n_0$ with $p divides.not n_0$. The inner sum is $sum_(m divides n_0) mu(m)$, which equals $1$ if $n_0 = 1$ (i.e., $n = p^k$) and $0$ otherwise. Hence $log P(X) = sum_(k >= 0) X^(p^k) \/ p^k$, so $E_p (X) = P(X)$. Since $m$ is coprime to $p$, the exponent $-mu(m) \/ m$ lies in $bb(Z)_p$. By the binomial theorem, $(1 - X^m)^alpha = sum_(j >= 0) binom(alpha, j) (-X^m)^j$ has coefficients in $bb(Z)_p$ for every $alpha in bb(Z)_p$ (as $binom(alpha, j) in bb(Z)_p$ by continuity of integer-valued polynomials from $bb(Z)$ to $bb(Z)_p$). Therefore $E_p (X) in bb(Z)_p[[X]]$.

    *Proof 2 (Dwork's Lemma).* Recall *Dwork's Lemma* (Exercise 8.8): a power series $F(X) in 1 + X bb(Q)_p[[X]]$ lies in $bb(Z)_p[[X]]$ if and only if $F(X^p) \/ F(X)^p in 1 + p X bb(Z)_p[[X]]$. For $F(X) = E_p (X)$, the exponents telescope completely:
    $ E_p (X^p) / E_p (X)^p = exp(sum_(k >= 0) X^(p^(k + 1)) / p^k - p sum_(k >= 0) X^(p^k) / p^k) = exp(-p X). $
    Because $v_p (p^n \/ n!) = n - (n - s_p (n)) \/ (p - 1) = (n(p - 2) + s_p (n)) \/ (p - 1) >= 1$ for every $n >= 1$ (for $p = 2$, $v_2 (2^n \/ n!) = s_2 (n) >= 1$), we have $exp(-p X) in 1 + p X bb(Z)_p[[X]]$. Dwork's Lemma immediately yields $E_p (X) in bb(Z)_p[[X]]$.
  ]

  #remark(title: "Failure of Ordinary Integrality for Height-2 Exponentials")[
    By contrast with $E_p (X)$, the naive composition $exp(log_(F_p)(X)) = exp(X + X^(p^2) \/ p + X^(p^4) \/ p^2 + dots.c)$ is *not* $p$-adically integral: its coefficient of $X^p$ is $1 \/ p!$, which has $p$-adic valuation $-1$ because there is no $X^p \/ p$ term inside the logarithm to cancel the pole of $exp(X)$! It is precisely this missing $X^p \/ p$ term (and all odd powers $X^(p^(2 k + 1)) \/ p^(2 k + 1)$) that makes the *formal* exponential $exp_(F_p)(z)$ converge on a strictly larger $p$-adic disk than $exp(z) - 1$.
  ]

  #insight-box(title: "Why Height 1 vs. Height 2 Diverge So Dramatically")[
    The contrast we just uncovered in the proof of Theorem 8.16 is the *exact* mechanism behind the entire Alpöge–Claude local divisibility bound!
    - For $hat(bb(G))_m$ (height $1$), the formal logarithm is $log(1 + T) = sum (-1)^(n - 1) T^n \/ n$, whose $p$-typical part is $sum_(k >= 0) T^(p^k) \/ p^k$. Its inverse—the ordinary exponential $e^z - 1 = sum z^n \/ n!$—has coefficients $1 \/ n!$ with $v_p (1 \/ n!) = -(n - s_p (n)) \/ (p - 1)$, matching the $1 \/ n!$ in Taylor's theorem $f(e^z - 1) = sum (D^n f)(0) z^n \/ n!$ and leaving *zero* net $p$-adic divisibility for $(D^n f)(0)$!
    - For a supersingular CM curve $hat(E)$ (height $2$), the formal logarithm $log_(F_p) (T) = sum_(k >= 0) T^(p^(2 k)) \/ p^k$ has *gaps* at all odd powers $p, p^3, p^5, dots.c$! Consequently, its inverse $exp_(F_p) (z) = sum c_a z^a$ has *much smaller* denominators: $v_p (c_a) >= -(a - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$ instead of $-(a - 1) \/ (p - 1)$! When we multiply by $a!$ to extract $(D^a f)(0)$, the difference between $v_p (a!) approx a \/ (p - 1)$ and $a \/ (p^2 - 1)$ survives as a *massive* surplus of $p$-adic divisibility:
      $ a / (p - 1) - a / (p^2 - 1) = (a p) / (p^2 - 1) = a / p + a / p^3 + a / p^5 + dots.c ! $
  ]

  Meanwhile, the Artin–Hasse exponential $E_p (X)$ remains indispensable for the general theory: it furnishes the canonical isomorphism between $p$-typical Witt vectors and the functor of $p$-typical curves on $hat(bb(G))_m$, controls the $p$-adic integrality of symmetric power series (`Formalism/ArtinHasse/ArtinHasseProofs/SymmSeries.lean`), and builds the Dieudonné module comparison between formal groups and crystalline cohomology.

  // ==========================================================================
  // 8.4 The Invariant Derivation D, Supersingular Vanishing, and p-Adic Divisibility
  // ==========================================================================
  == The Invariant Derivation $D$, Supersingular Vanishing $D^p equiv 0$, and $p$-Adic Divisibility

  We now arrive at the centerpiece of this chapter (corresponding directly to Item 2 of Alpöge's *Remark*). We prove *both* the first-order supersingular vanishing $D^p equiv 0 (mod p)$ and the sharp all-orders $p$-adic divisibility theorem for $D^a theta(0) \/ Omega^a$.

  === The Invariant Derivation and Supersingular Vanishing $D^p equiv 0 (mod p)$

  Let $cal(E)$ be a smooth elliptic curve over $cal(O)_(K_p)$ with good reduction $tilde(E)$ over $bb(F)_(p^2)$, and let $hat(E)$ be its formal group in a local parameter $T$ at the origin $O$. Let $omega = log'_(hat(E)) (T) d T$ be the normalized invariant differential, where $log_(hat(E)) (T) = sum_(n >= 1) c_n T^n \/ n$ with $c_1 = 1$ and $c_n in cal(O)_(K_p)$.

  #definition(title: "Invariant Derivation")[
    The *invariant derivation* $D$ on $hat(E)$ (equivalently, on $cal(E)$) is the unique $cal(O)_(K_p)$-linear derivation of $cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ dual to $omega$, namely
    $ D = (log'_(hat(E)) (T))^(-1) d / (d T) : cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]] -> cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]. $
    Equivalently, in the formal logarithm coordinate $z = log_(hat(E)) (T)$, we have $D = d \/ d z$.
  ]

  Recall that for any derivation $D$ on a commutative ring $A$ of characteristic $p$, the $p$-th iterate $D^p$ is *again* a derivation of $A$: by the Leibniz rule,
  $ D^p (f g) = sum_(k=0)^p binom(p, k) (D^k f)(D^(p - k) g) equiv (D^p f) g + f (D^p g) (mod p), $
  since $p divides binom(p, k)$ for all $0 < k < p$. Furthermore, because $D$ commutes with translation on the formal group $hat(E)$, so does $D^p$. Since the $bb(F)_(p^2)$-vector space of translation-invariant derivations on $hat(E)_(bb(F)_(p^2))$ is one-dimensional (spanned by $D$), there must exist a unique scalar $A in bb(F)_(p^2)$—the *Hasse invariant* of $tilde(E)$—such that
  $ D^p equiv A dot.c D (mod p) quad text("on ") cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]. $

  #theorem(title: "Identification of the Hasse Invariant and Supersingular Vanishing")[
    Let $cal(E) \/ cal(O)_(K_p)$ be an elliptic curve with good reduction $tilde(E) \/ bb(F)_(p^2)$ and formal logarithm $log_(hat(E)) (T) = sum_(n >= 1) c_n T^n \/ n$.
    + The Hasse invariant $A in bb(F)_(p^2)$ satisfies $A equiv c_p (mod p)$, so
      $ D^p equiv c_p dot.c D (mod p) quad text("on ") cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]. $
    + Moreover, if $[p]_(hat(E)) (T) = sum_(n >= 1) a_n T^n in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$, then $a_p equiv c_p (mod p)$.
    + In particular, when $E$ has complex multiplication by $cal(O)_K$ and $p$ is *inert* in $K$, the reduction $tilde(E)$ is supersingular ($hat(E)$ has height $2$), so $a_p equiv c_p equiv 0 (mod p)$ and therefore
      $ D^p equiv 0 (mod p) quad text("on ") cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]. $
  ]

  #proof[
    *(1)* Truncate the formal logarithm at degree $p$ by writing
    $ g(T) = sum_(n=1)^p c_n T^n / n = h(T) + c_p T^p / p, quad text("where ") h(T) = sum_(n=1)^(p-1) c_n T^n / n. $
    Because $1, 2, dots.c, p - 1$ are units in $cal(O)_(K_p)$, we have $h(T) in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ with $h(0) = 0$ and $h'(0) = c_1 = 1$. Now compute $D(g(T))$:
    $ D(g(T)) = (g'(T)) / (log'_(hat(E)) (T)) = (sum_(n=1)^p c_n T^(n-1)) / (sum_(n >= 1) c_n T^(n-1)) = 1 + O(T^p). $
    Since $D$ lowers the $T$-adic order of a power series by at most $1$ and annihilates constants, applying $D^(p - 1)$ to $1 + O(T^p)$ gives $D^p (g(T)) in O(T)$, so
    $ (D^p g)(0) = 0. $
    On the other hand, we compute $(D^p h)(0)$ and $(D^p (T^p))(0)$ separately:
    - Since $D^p equiv A dot.c D (mod p)$ on $cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$, evaluating at $T = 0$ gives
      $ (D^p h)(0) equiv A dot.c (D h)(0) = A dot.c h'(0) = A (mod p). $
    - Since $D(T) = (log'_(hat(E))(T))^(-1) = 1 + O(T)$, an immediate induction on $k = 0, 1, dots.c, p$ shows that
      $ D^k (T^p) = p(p - 1) dots.c (p - k + 1) T^(p - k) + O(T^(p - k + 1)). $
      Setting $k = p$ and $T = 0$ gives $(D^p (T^p))(0) = p!$.

    Combining these with $(D^p g)(0) = 0$ yields
    $ 0 = (D^p g)(0) = (D^p h)(0) + c_p (p - 1)! equiv A - c_p (mod p), $
    where we used Wilson's theorem $(p - 1)! equiv -1 (mod p)$. Thus $A equiv c_p (mod p)$.

    *(2)* Compare the coefficient of $T^p$ on both sides of the identity $log_(hat(E)) ([p]_(hat(E)) (T)) = p log_(hat(E)) (T)$. On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $T^p$ is $p dot.c (c_p \/ p) = c_p$. On the left-hand side, because $[p]_(hat(E)) (T)$ reduces modulo $p$ to a power series in $T^p$, we have $p divides a_m$ for all $1 <= m <= p - 1$. Expanding
    $ log_(hat(E)) ([p]_(hat(E)) (T)) &= [p]_(hat(E)) (T) + sum_(n=2)^(p-1) c_n / n ([p]_(hat(E)) (T))^n \
      & quad + c_p / p ([p]_(hat(E)) (T))^p + O(T^(p + 1)), $
    every term with $2 <= n <= p - 1$ has $n in cal(O)_(K_p)^times$ and contributes a multiple of $p^2$ to the coefficient of $T^p$; the $n = p$ term contributes $(c_p \/ p) a_1^p = c_p p^(p - 1) equiv 0 (mod p)$ (since $a_1 = p$ and $p >= 2$). Thus the coefficient of $T^p$ on the left-hand side is congruent to $a_p (mod p)$, giving $a_p equiv c_p (mod p)$.

    *(3)* When $p$ is inert in $K$, Deuring's Criterion (Theorem 8.7) implies that $tilde(E)$ is supersingular, so $hat(E)$ has height $2$ and $[p]_(hat(E)) (T) equiv u T^(p^2) + O(T^(p^2 + 1)) (mod p)$. In particular $a_p equiv 0 (mod p)$, hence $c_p equiv 0 (mod p)$ and $D^p equiv 0 (mod p)$.
  ]

  The congruence $D^p equiv 0 (mod p)$ already implies by induction that $D^(p k) equiv 0 (mod p^k)$ on $cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$, giving the preliminary bound $v_p ((D^a f)(0)) >= floor(a \/ p) = a \/ p - O(1)$ for every $f in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$. Yet the height-$2$ formal group structure gives *strictly more*—an infinite geometric series of $p$-adic divisibility!

  === The Sharp $p$-Adic Divisibility Theorem at Inert Primes

  #lemma(title: "Denominators of the Height-2 Formal Exponential")[
    Let $F_p$ be the canonical height-$2$ Lubin–Tate formal group over $bb(Z)_p$ with logarithm $log_(F_p) (T) = sum_(k >= 0) T^(p^(2 k)) \/ p^k$, and write its formal exponential as
    $ exp_(F_p) (z) = sum_(a >= 1) b_a z^a in bb(Q)_p[[z]]. $
    Then for every integer $a >= 1$,
    $ v_p (b_a) >= -(a - 1) / (p^2 - 1). $
    Consequently, for every formal group $hat(E)$ over $cal(O)_(K_p)$ of height $2$ (strictly isomorphic over $W(overline(bb(F))_p)$ to $F_p$) and every integral power series $f(T) in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$, the Taylor expansion $f(exp_(hat(E)) (z)) = sum_(a >= 0) d_a z^a$ satisfies
    $ v_p (d_a) >= -(a - 1) / (p^2 - 1) quad text("for all ") a >= 1. $
  ]

  #proof[
    From $log_(F_p) (exp_(F_p) (z)) = z$, we have the recursive identity
    $ exp_(F_p) (z) = z - sum_(k >= 1) 1 / p^k (exp_(F_p) (z))^(p^(2 k)). $
    We prove $v_p (b_a) >= -(a - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$ by strong induction on $a >= 1$. For $a = 1$, $b_1 = 1$, so $v_p (b_1) = 0 = -(1 - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$. Now let $a >= 2$ and assume the bound holds for all $1 <= j < a$. Extracting the coefficient of $z^a$ from the recursion expresses $b_a$ as a finite sum of terms of the form
    $ -1 / p^k b_(j_1) b_(j_2) dots.c b_(j_(p^(2 k))) $
    with $k >= 1$, $j_i >= 1$, and $j_1 + j_2 + dots.c + j_(p^(2 k)) = a$. Note that $j_i <= a - (p^(2 k) - 1) < a$ for every $i$, so the induction hypothesis applies to every factor $b_(j_i)$. The $p$-adic valuation of each such term is at least
    $ -k - sum_(i=1)^(p^(2 k)) (j_i - 1) / (p^2 - 1) = -k - (a - p^(2 k)) / (p^2 - 1) = -(a - 1) / (p^2 - 1) + ((p^(2 k) - 1) / (p^2 - 1) - k). $
    Since $(p^(2 k) - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1) = 1 + p^2 + p^4 + dots.c + p^(2 k - 2) >= k$ for every integer $k >= 1$ (with equality at $k = 1$), the second term is nonnegative! By the ultrametric triangle inequality, $v_p (b_a) >= -(a - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$, completing the induction.

    Now let $hat(E)$ be any height-$2$ formal group over $cal(O)_(K_p)$, and let $phi(T) = T + O(T^2) in W(overline(bb(F))_p)[[T]]$ be a strict isomorphism $hat(E) ->^(tilde.equiv) F_p$ (Corollary 8.15). Then $exp_(hat(E)) (z) = phi^(-1)(exp_(F_p)(z))$. Given $f(T) in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$, set $tilde(f)(U) = f(phi^(-1)(U)) = sum_(m >= 0) f_m U^m in W(overline(bb(F))_p)[[U]]$, so $v_p (f_m) >= 0$ for all $m >= 0$. For $a >= 1$, the coefficient $d_a$ of $z^a$ in $f(exp_(hat(E))(z)) = tilde(f)(exp_(F_p)(z))$ is a sum of terms $f_m b_(j_1) dots.c b_(j_m)$ with $m >= 1$, $j_i >= 1$, and $j_1 + dots.c + j_m = a$. Its $p$-adic valuation is at least
    $ v_p (f_m) - sum_(i=1)^m (j_i - 1) / (p^2 - 1) >= -(a - m) / (p^2 - 1) >= -(a - 1) / (p^2 - 1), $
    since $m >= 1$. This completes the proof.
  ]

  We now combine Lemma 8.20 with Legendre's formula for $v_p (a!)$ to obtain the fundamental local divisibility theorem!

  #theorem(title: "Fundamental Local Divisibility at Inert Primes")[
    Let $E$ be an elliptic curve with complex multiplication by $cal(O)_K$ having good reduction at a prime $frak(P)$ of $H_K$ lying over a rational prime $p$ that is *inert* in $K$. Let $D$ be the invariant derivation on the smooth integral model $cal(E) \/ cal(O)_(K_p)$, and let $f in cal(O)_(K_p)[[T]]$ be any function regular on the formal neighborhood of the origin (for instance, a normalized theta function $theta$ whose Taylor series in $T$ is $p$-adically integral, so $(D^a f)(0) = D^a theta(0) \/ Omega^a$). Then for every integer $a >= 1$,
    $ v_p ((D^a f)(0)) >= (a p) / (p^2 - 1) - (s_p (a)) / (p - 1) + 1 / (p^2 - 1), $
    where $s_p (a)$ is the sum of the base-$p$ digits of $a$. In particular, expanding $p \/ (p^2 - 1) = sum_(k >= 0) p^(-(2 k + 1))$ and using $s_p (a) <= (p - 1)(floor(log_p a) + 1) = O(log a)$, we obtain the sharp estimate
    $ v_p (D^a theta(0) / Omega^a) >= a / p + a / p^3 + a / p^5 + dots.c - O(log a) = (a p) / (p^2 - 1) - O(log a). $
  ]

  #proof[
    Because $D = d \/ d z$ in the formal logarithm coordinate $z = log_(hat(E)) (T)$, Taylor's theorem gives
    $ f(exp_(hat(E)) (z)) = sum_(a >= 0) (D^a f)(0) z^a / a! . $
    Thus $(D^a f)(0) = a! dot.c d_a$, where $d_a = [z^a] f(exp_(hat(E))(z))$. By Legendre's formula,
    $ v_p (a!) = sum_(j >= 1) floor(a / p^j) = (a - s_p (a)) / (p - 1). $
    Applying Lemma 8.20 gives $v_p (d_a) >= -(a - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$. Adding the two valuations yields
    $ v_p ((D^a f)(0)) = v_p (a!) + v_p (d_a) >= (a - s_p (a)) / (p - 1) - (a - 1) / (p^2 - 1). $
    Since
    $ 1 / (p - 1) - 1 / (p^2 - 1) = (p + 1 - 1) / (p^2 - 1) = p / (p^2 - 1) = 1 / p + 1 / p^3 + 1 / p^5 + dots.c, $
    we immediately obtain
    $ v_p ((D^a f)(0)) >= (a p) / (p^2 - 1) - (s_p (a)) / (p - 1) + 1 / (p^2 - 1). $
    Finally, since $a$ has $floor(log_p a) + 1$ digits in base $p$, each at most $p - 1$, we have $s_p (a) \/ (p - 1) <= floor(log_p a) + 1 = O(log a)$, proving the theorem.
  ]

  #remark(title: "Sharpness of the Exponent and Comparison with Ordinary Primes")[
    The bound in Theorem 8.21 is *asymptotically sharp*! Indeed, for $f(T) = T$ on $F_p$ and $a = p^(2 k)$, the leading coefficient of $exp_(F_p)(z)$ at $z^(p^(2 k))$ achieves $v_p (b_(p^(2 k))) = -(p^(2 k) - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$ (for $k = 1$, $b_(p^2) = -1 \/ p$ has valuation $-1 = -(p^2 - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1)$), so $v_p ((D^(p^(2 k)) T)(0)) = (p^(2 k) p - 1) \/ (p^2 - 1) - 1 \/ (p - 1)$ matches the lower bound up to $O(1)$.

    By contrast, at a *split* prime $p$ (height $1$), the same induction gives $v_p (b_a) >= -(a - 1) \/ (p - 1)$, which completely cancels $v_p (a!) = (a - s_p (a)) \/ (p - 1)$ and leaves only $O(1)$! Thus *only* the inert (supersingular) primes generate exponential $p$-adic divisibility under repeated application of $D$.
  ]

  #insight-box(title: "How Inert-Prime Divisibility Manufactures 'Stepanov Multiplicity'")[
    Why is Theorem 8.21 the arithmetic engine of the Alpöge–Claude proof? Recall from Chapter 3 that Stepanov's method over $bb(F)_q$ succeeds by constructing a nonzero auxiliary function $Phi$ whose order of vanishing at a large set of points exceeds its degree, forcing a contradiction unless the point count obeys the Weil bound. Over $bb(F)_q$, one attains multiplicity $m$ by working with the Hasse–Schmidt derivatives $D^a \/ a!$ for $a < m$, using the miracle that $D^p = 0$ keeps degrees small.

    Over $bb(Z)$, we cannot force a nonzero integer to vanish to order $m$ at a prime $p$—*unless* we can prove that the integer is divisible by a high power $p^M$! Theorem 8.21 shows that differentiating a CM theta function $a$ times via the invariant derivation $D$ and normalizing by $Omega^a$ produces an algebraic integer whose $p$-adic valuation at *every* inert prime $p$ is at least $a p \/ (p^2 - 1) - O(log a)$. Multiplying across all inert primes $p$ in a range (or in an Iwasawa/automorphic family) manufactures a massive global integer denominator/divisor—an *Arakelov multiplicity*—that plays the exact role of Stepanov's vanishing divisor when fed into Bost's slope inequality (Chapter 9)!
  ]

  // ==========================================================================
  // 8.5 Extension to Higher-Rank Hermitian Theta Series and Weyl Branching
  // ==========================================================================
  == Extension to Higher-Rank Hermitian Theta Series and Weyl Branching

  The one-dimensional CM elliptic curve setting of Sections 8.1–8.4 is the prototype for the higher-rank automorphic machinery used in Part III of the proof. As Alpöge recounts in his *Remark* (Items 3, 8, and 9), winning multiplicity $m approx sqrt(X)$ in a natively automorphic manner requires passing from a single CM elliptic curve to *iterated theta lifts* along a chain of totally definite unitary groups
  $ U(X_0) -> U(X_1) -> dots.c -> U(X_m), $
  where each $X_j$ is a positive-definite Hermitian space over the CM field $K$ (or a CM extension $K \/ K_0$). In this section we outline how the three pillars—CM periods (Chowla–Selberg), formal-group / Weyl divisibility, and lattice covolumes (Siegel mass)—lift from rank $1$ to higher rank.

  === CM Abelian Varieties and Pluriharmonic Theta Series

  Let $E$ be a CM elliptic curve with $op("End")(E) tilde.equiv cal(O)_K$, and let $(L, chevron.l dot.c, dot.c chevron.r)$ be a positive-definite Hermitian $cal(O)_K$-lattice of rank $r$. The $r$-dimensional CM abelian variety $A = E^r tilde.equiv bb(C)^r \/ (Omega L)$ inherits an action of $cal(O)_K$ and a principal (or polarized) Hermitian form from $L$. Its theta series, evaluated at CM points, generalizes the classical Jacobi theta function: for a pluriharmonic polynomial $P$ of weight $nu$ (an irreducible representation of $op("GL")_r$ or $U(r)$), the associated Hermitian theta series is
  $ Theta_(L, P) (tau) = sum_(v in L) P(v) e^(pi i tau chevron.l v, v chevron.r). $
  In the Weil representation (Chapter 7), the polynomial $P(v)$ arises precisely by applying a *pluriharmonic differential operator*—the higher-rank analogue of the iterated invariant derivation $D^a$—to the Gaussian/theta kernel at the origin!

  When $p$ is inert in $K$, the formal group $hat(A) tilde.equiv hat(E)^r$ is an $r$-dimensional formal $cal(O)_(K_p)$-module of height $2 r$ (all slopes equal to $1 \/ 2$). The multidimensional Hazewinkel Functional Equation Lemma (`Formalism.FG.FunEqHomV`) guarantees that $hat(A)$ is strictly isomorphic over $W(overline(bb(F))_p)$ to $F_p^r$, so *every* partial invariant derivation $D_1, dots.c, D_r$ along the $r$ coordinates satisfies $D_j^p equiv 0 (mod p)$ and obeys the same $p$-adic divisibility law as in Theorem 8.21!

  === Weyl Modules, Dual Weyl Modules, and Graded Weyl Divisibility

  In rank $r = 1$, the differential operator of degree $a$ is simply $D^a$: there is only one representation of $op("GL")_1$ of weight $a$, and its integral structure over $bb(Z)_p$ is trivial (rank $1$). In rank $r >= 2$, however, the space of homogeneous polynomials of degree $a$ in $r times n$ matrix variables decomposes under $op("GL")_r times op("GL")_n$ (Howe duality / Cauchy identity) into irreducible representations indexed by partitions (Young diagrams) $nu = (nu_1 >= nu_2 >= dots.c >= nu_n >= 0)$ of size $|nu| = a$.

  Over $bb(C)$, the irreducible representation $V_nu$ of $op("GL")_r$ is unique up to isomorphism. Over $bb(Z)$ or $bb(Z)_p$, however, there are *two* canonical, non-isomorphic integral forms of $V_nu$:
  + The *dual Weyl module* (or Schur module / induced module) $nabla(nu)$ (`Formalism.GradedWeylDivisibility.dualWeyl`), defined as the space of highest-weight polynomials in $R[x_(i, j)]$ (i.e., $U_n$-invariants of column weight $nu$), spanned integrally by *bideterminants* (`bideterminant`)—products of initial minors associated to semistandard Young tableaux of shape $nu$.
  + The *Weyl module* $Delta(nu)$ (`Formalism.GradedWeylDivisibility.weyl`), generated over $R$ by applying the Kostant divided-power operators $E_(a, b)^((k)) = E_(a, b)^k \/ k!$ (`rowDiv`, `applyWord`) to the canonical highest-weight vector $op("hw")(nu) = product_(s=1)^n det_s^(nu_s - nu_(s+1))$.

  There is a canonical $op("GL")_r (bb(Z))$-equivariant map $Delta(nu) -> nabla(nu)$ that becomes an isomorphism after tensoring with $bb(Q)$, but over $bb(Z)_p$ its *cokernel* is finite torsion! In the Alpöge–Claude proof, the theta-lift differential operators naturally land in the *Weyl module* (via divided powers $D^a \/ a!$ and Kostant operators) while the integral lattice theta values naturally pair against the *dual Weyl module* (via integral bideterminants). The $p$-adic index of $Delta(nu)$ inside $nabla(nu)$—together with the *Weyl branching law* restricting representations from $U(X_(j+1))$ to $U(X_j)$ (which enforces the GGP interlacing weights of Item 7 in *Remark*)—governs the exact $p$-adic divisibility of the order-$m$ terms in the $m$-fold iterated theta lift!

  === The Siegel Mass Formula and Global Denominator Control

  Local $p$-adic divisibility at inert primes is only half of the global height balance: one must also bound the *global denominators* introduced when passing from a single Hermitian lattice $L$ to a global automorphic form on the finite double coset space
  $ U(X)(K_0) backslash U(X)(bb(A)_(K_0, f)) \/ K_f = union.sq_(i=1)^H { [L_i] }, $
  which parametrizes the *genus* of $L$. Each lattice class $[L_i]$ carries a finite automorphism group $Gamma_i = op("Aut")(L_i)$, and the Petersson inner product on $U(X)$ weights each class by $1 \/ |op("Aut")(L_i)|$. The total mass of the genus is given by the *Siegel–Weil / Siegel mass formula*:
  $ op("Mass")(op("gen")(L)) := sum_(i=1)^H 1 / |op("Aut")(L_i)| = tau(U(X)) dot.c product_v (alpha_v (L))^(-1), $
  where $tau(U(X)) = 2$ is the Tamagawa number of the unitary group $U(X)$ and $alpha_v (L)$ are the local densities at all places $v$ of $K_0$ (expressed at unramified places in terms of special values of $L$-functions $L(k, chi_K^k)$!).

  Putting the three ingredients together (Item 9 of *Remark*):
  + *Chowla–Selberg* supplies the archimedean period normalization $Omega^a$ for the derivatives of CM theta series;
  + *Formal-group vanishing and Graded Weyl divisibility* supply the local $p$-adic divisibility at inert primes for the iterated theta lifts;
  + *Siegel mass* controls the covolumes and denominators of the Petersson norms of the lifts across the genus of $X_j$.

  Together, these three pillars deliver the proven divisibility lower bound on the $m$-fold iterated lift that powers the master divisor-slope inequality in Part III!

  // ==========================================================================
  // 8.6 Lean 4 Formalization
  // ==========================================================================
  == Lean 4 Formalization

  The theory developed in this chapter is extensively formalized in `leanprover-community/riemann-hypothesis`, spanning both the `Formalism` library (foundational formal groups, Witt vectors, Artin–Hasse exponentials, and graded Weyl modules) and the `Development` libraries (complex multiplication, Chowla–Selberg periods, and crystalline periods of abelian varieties).

  #lean-note(
    title: "Lean 4 Formalization: CM Curves, Formal Groups, and Weyl Divisibility",
    module: "Formalism.* & Development.AbelianVarietyTheory",
  )[
    - *Formal Group Logarithms and Hazewinkel's Functional Equation* (`Formalism/FormalGroupLog/Statement.lean`, `LogDeterminesLaw.lean`):
      - `Formalism.G.IsHom`, `Formalism.G.IsLog`: defines homomorphisms and formal logarithms of one-dimensional formal groups over an arbitrary commutative ring `R` (building on Mathlib's `FormalGroup`).
      - `Formalism.G.LogExistsUnique`: proves existence and uniqueness of `log_F` over any `ℚ`-algebra `R`.
      - `Formalism.G.FunctionalEquationSetting`, `IsFunEqType`, `FunEqGroup`, `FunEqHom`: formalizes Theorem 8.14 (Hazewinkel's Functional Equation Lemma), proving integrality of both the formal group law $f^(-1)(f(X) + f(Y))$ and homomorphisms $f^(-1)(g(T))$ over a complete ultrametric field $K$ with Frobenius lift $sigma$.
    - *Multidimensional Formal Groups and Witt Vectors* (`Formalism/FormalGroupWitt/Statement.lean`, `WeightInvariance.lean`, `SubstInjectiveV.lean`):
      - `Formalism.FG.IsFunEqTypeV`, `FunEqHomV`: lifts Hazewinkel's functional equation lemma to arbitrary finite dimension `ι` with an integral Frobenius matrix `M : ι → ι → K`.
      - `Formalism.FG.zlToW`, `WittCharZero`, `WittTeichRoot`: constructs the canonical embedding $bb(Z)_ell -> W(overline(bb(F))_ell)$, establishes characteristic zero for $W(overline(bb(F))_ell)$, and proves the existence of primitive $(ell^d - 1)$-st Teichmüller roots of unity.
    - *Artin–Hasse Exponential and Symmetric Series* (`Formalism/ArtinHasse/ArtinHasseProofs/SymmSeries.lean`):
      - Formalizes the $p$-adic integrality of the Artin–Hasse exponential and symmetric power series (`Formalism.FT.SymmetricSeries`), bridging Witt vectors and $p$-typical formal exponentials.
    - *Graded Weyl Divisibility* (`Formalism/GradedWeylDivisibility/StatementA.lean`–`StatementE.lean`, `DualWeylDominanceOf.lean`, `FinalB.lean`–`FinalD.lean`):
      - `Formalism.GradedWeylDivisibility.dualWeyl`, `weyl`, `bideterminant`, `hw`, `rowDiv`, `applyWord`: defines the integral dual Weyl module $nabla(nu)$ (via column invariants and bideterminants) and Weyl module $Delta(nu)$ (via divided-power row operators applied to highest-weight minors), and proves the graded divisibility and dominance theorems used to control iterated theta lifts.
    - *Complex Multiplication, Ray Class Fields, and Chowla–Selberg* (`Development/AbelianVarietyTheory` & `Development/AutomorphicSpectrum`):
      - `GlobalTheory.Published.EllipticMainTheoremCM` (`Development/AutomorphicSpectrum/Proofs/GlobalProofs/EllipticMainTheoremCM.lean`): proves the idelic Main Theorem of Complex Multiplication (Theorem 8.4).
      - `GlobalTheory.Published.HilbertClassFieldJ` (`HilbertClassFieldJ.lean`) and `EquidistributedIdealClassesOfHilbertClassFieldJ`: proves that $H_K = K(j(E))$ is the Hilbert class field of $K$.
      - `GlobalTheory.Published.WeberRayFieldSt` (`Development/AbelianVarietyTheory/PublishedTheorems/WeberRayClassFields/WeberRayFieldSt.lean`): proves that adjoining the Weber values `weberSet L n` generates the ray class field modulo $(n)$.
      - `MeanLogPeriodChowlaSelberg`, `MeanLogPeriodConjugatesEqChowlaSelberg`, `ChowlaSelbergPeriod`: formalizes the Chowla–Selberg period formula (Theorem 8.9) and its Galois-conjugate mean over ideal classes.
  ]

